{"connection_id": "BC-7806198e84c195f88fdb", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.24", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Collective pledge to serve and obey the Lord", "note": "The commentary hears the owned servant speak directly to the owner in the first-person plural; Israel here answers in a collective pledge to serve the Lord and obey his voice, adding a covenantal setting for that spoken allegiance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-60c53f3506fa67c2fac2", "ref": "WLC:Lev.25.42", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Israelites are God's servants and not human property", "note": "The commentary says all people belong to God whether free or enslaved; Leviticus grounds Israel's status as God's servants in the exodus and bars their sale as slaves, limiting human ownership by God's prior claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0dbcc1519d40c8dc6717", "ref": "WLC:Lev.25.42", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Israel belongs to God, not sale as slaves", "note": "Because Israelites are God's servants brought from Egypt, they may not be sold in a slave sale; it qualifies the owner-slave analogy by denying an Israelite human master an absolute claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff161c29b4ee388e7a0f", "ref": "WLC:Lev.25.43", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "do not rule ruthlessly over a servant", "note": "The law prohibits ruthless rule over an impoverished Israelite servant and requires fear of God, placing a divine limit on the domination developed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-66e6a28c8b60def70e75", "ref": "WLC:Lev.25.46", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "slaves as inherited possession", "note": "The law permits inherited, permanent possession of foreign slaves, a stark legal counterpart to the commentary's technical treatment of a mamlūk as property."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b2d437a5f2bb5428a0f1", "ref": "WLC:Lev.25.55", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "“My servants” and deliverance from Egypt", "note": "God twice calls Israelites “my servants”; the verse makes divine ownership the reason they are not ultimately servants of human owners."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c3f7312c5c385907edb2", "ref": "WLC:Mal.1.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "servant honors master, God as master", "note": "Malachi argues from a servant's honor for a master to God's claim on Israel's honor, directly using household hierarchy to define proper reverence for God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b0a2ef78fa60dea1099c", "ref": "WLC:Prov.17.2", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "wise servant shares a son's inheritance", "note": "A prudent servant rules over a shameful son and shares the inheritance with the brothers, overturning a fixed hierarchy of owner, household, and slave."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d777d6a4d4e4e108fa61", "ref": "WLC:Prov.27.18", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one guarding a master is honored", "note": "Proverbs says the one who keeps or attends to a master will be honored, a compact wisdom parallel to the commentary's secondary possibility that service and honor meet."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8231a9829c06f0f6f581", "ref": "WLC:Ps.100.2", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "serve God with gladness", "note": "The liturgical command to serve YHWH joyfully gives a cultic form of service that is more than inward feeling, while changing the commentary's stress on extreme abasement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e71a0a97b33e3740245", "ref": "WLC:Ps.100.3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "made by God, his people and flock", "note": "WLC's ketiv reads that God made us “and not we [ourselves],” while its qere reads “and we are his”; the verse then calls the people his and the flock of his pasture, so both streams need to be kept distinct in this creation-and-belonging parallel."}

