{"connection_id": "BC-ba0219ac54775e536fc7", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "source ر ح م: fatherly compassion for children", "note": "The psalm compares YHWH's compassion for those who fear him to a father's compassion for children, using the r-ḥ-m root. It gives a paternal rendering of the commentary's Rahman/Rahim frame around nurturing lordship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6664674042895bd9d791", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.14", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "fatherly raḥamim", "note": "The commentary’s Arabic mercy root corresponds to Hebrew r-ḥ-m in a verse comparing God’s compassion to a father’s compassion for children, giving the lexical link a concrete parental setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a1bbb9f7d5783ed2fd66", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.14", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "father and children", "note": "The commentary’s womb-to-parental-care frame meets the psalm’s explicit image of God’s compassion like a father’s for children; it interprets mercy as a sustained relation, not an abstract quality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff947616828bd0f396d6", "ref": "WLC:Ps.127.4", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "children as fruit of the womb", "note": "The commentary begins its frame with the womb as the child’s home; the psalm calls children an inheritance from God and the fruit of the womb a reward, assigning value to the life that grows there."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ce36243a696a419e7ed", "ref": "WLC:Ps.131.2", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "quieted soul like a weaned child with its mother", "note": "The psalm compares its quieted soul to a weaned child with its mother. It adds the post-nursing stage of peaceful attachment to the commentary's womb, breast, and gradual completion sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e4bdc4891a83098b186a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.131.3", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a weaned child with its mother", "note": "The commentary describes a child’s growth inside a home and the transition toward completion; the psalm compares its quieted soul to a weaned child beside its mother, where dependence has changed but maternal closeness remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4109013649af63592d4c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.133.2", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "n-ʿ-m / pleasant kinship", "note": "The commentary labels Arabic ن ع م and glosses it with raising children in comfort; Hebrew naʿim is a cognate meaning pleasant, used here of brothers dwelling together, so the source’s comfort image meets a concrete kin household."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb9cdba1e72c20ca6a2c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God forms within the mother's womb", "note": "The psalm credits God with forming the inward parts and weaving the speaker in the mother's womb. It directly accompanies the commentary's Qur'an 3:6 image of God shaping a person in the rahim as a place of formation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d8497a807ae3215af99", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.14", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "formed in the mother’s womb", "note": "The commentary makes the womb a place where a child grows and is formed; the psalm says God knit the speaker together in his mother’s womb, locating divine making within that home."}
{"connection_id": "BC-53ddacba0a1945605f1f", "ref": "WLC:Ps.22.10", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "womb and mother’s breasts", "note": "The commentary calls the womb a child’s first home and describes subsequent nurture; the psalmist says God took him from the womb and made him secure at his mother’s breasts."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6446784d7f517ef0c3df", "ref": "WLC:Ps.22.10", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God brings the speaker from womb to mother's breasts", "note": "The speaker credits God with drawing him from the womb and making him trust at his mother's breasts. It holds together God's hidden guardianship and maternal nourishment, the two agents the commentary distinguishes in Musa's infancy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50b5558a306abd7d9209", "ref": "WLC:Ps.22.11", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cast from the womb toward God", "note": "The commentary’s womb image is a home for the child; the psalm says the speaker was cast upon God from birth and relied on God from his mother’s womb, extending the image from maternal shelter to divine care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c6554da2914616d0a2c1", "ref": "WLC:Ps.27.10", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God receives the one abandoned by parents", "note": "Even if father and mother forsake the speaker, YHWH receives him. It provides a compact divine-foster-parent image beside the commentary's insistence that the one who truly raises a child may be other than the visible household."}
